Date: Sat, 6 Jun 2026 11:32:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 05/33] fix(verimark): set non-NULL username/description on
 list() prints

fprintd's `delete_enrolled_fingers` (called at the start of
`fprint_device_enroll_start` to clean stale prints) iterates the FpPrint
array our `list()` returns and logs metadata of each via g_log. With
NULL `fp_print_get_username` / `fp_print_get_description`, the format
string `"%s"` lands on a NULL pointer and strlen segfaults inside g_log.

Mirror the standard FpDevice convention: every FpPrint we emit carries
explicit non-NULL strings, even if they're synthetic ("verimark" /
"Kensington VeriMark DT (device-side enrollment)"). fprintd overrides
these from its user-cache when it matches the print back to an enrolled
finger.

Confirmed runtime: `fprintd-enroll` previously crashed in
`delete_enrolled_fingers + 0x1731d` before our enroll vfunc was even
called; the user only saw "EnrollStart failed: Remote peer disconnected".

Drafted with AI assistance.
---
 libfprint/drivers/verimark/verimark.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/libfprint/drivers/verimark/verimark.c b/libfprint/drivers/verimark/verimark.c
index 63368be..ac3cab2 100644
--- a/libfprint/drivers/verimark/verimark.c
+++ b/libfprint/drivers/verimark/verimark.c
@@ -1388,6 +1388,14 @@ verimark_print_from_tuid(FpDevice *device,
     fpi_print_set_type(print, FPI_PRINT_RAW);
     fpi_print_set_device_stored(print, TRUE);
     g_object_set(print, "fpi-data", data, NULL);
+    /* fprintd logs print metadata during enroll cleanup
+     * (`delete_enrolled_fingers`) and crashes if username / description
+     * are NULL — g_log "%s" on NULL faults in strlen. Set explicit
+     * non-NULL placeholders. The real username/finger get attached by
+     * fprintd from its user-cache when matching against this print. */
+    fp_print_set_username(print, "verimark");
+    fp_print_set_description(print, "Kensington VeriMark DT (device-side enrollment)");
+    fp_print_set_finger(print, FP_FINGER_UNKNOWN);
 
     return print;
 }
